IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `tfa-dates`

-- Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) -- the rules a member of the family of a person killed by a wrongful act meets: who is
-- a "member of the family" (s 3), the action that survives the death (s 4), for whose benefit and by whom it is brought
-- (ss 5, 8), the one-action rule (s 6), the particulars the plaintiff must deliver (s 7), payment into court (s 9), what
-- is left out of the damages and what they may include (s 10), and the division of the amount recovered (s 5). The whole
-- Act is in, apart from the short title (s 1) and s 2(1) (the Crown is bound; s 2(2) is in the description of s 4).
--
-- This module holds the law. The questions are asked in tfa-seat.l4, which IMPORTs it and never restates a rule.
-- Nothing here is exported.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no rule answers JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no ("means", "shall not",
-- "not more than one", a "whenever" or "if" condition that fails); or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on the facts
-- given, with a "NOTHING-kind" comment. Judgments the Act leaves to the court or the jury ("wrongful act, neglect or
-- default", "reasonable cost", whether the jury "think the sum sufficient", the shares the jury find) are inputs: the
-- answer is conditional on them, never a guess at them.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) State of Tasmania, CC BY
-- 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Fatal Accidents Act 1934`

§§ `s 3 -- member of the family`

-- s 3(1): "member of the family, in relation to a person, means that person's -- (a) spouse, partner, parent,
-- stepparent, grandparent, child, stepchild or grandchild; or (b) brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister";
-- "partner, in relation to a person, means an individual who, at the time when a wrongful act, neglect or default
-- occurred in respect of the person, was in a personal relationship, within the meaning of the Relationships Act 2003,
-- with the person". "Spouse", "child", "stepchild" and the rest are not defined here: asked as facts. Whether a
-- relationship is a "personal relationship" within the Relationships Act 2003 (not deposited) is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Relationship to the deceased` IS ONE OF
    `spouse`
    `partner in a personal relationship`
    `parent`
    `stepparent`
    `grandparent`
    `child`
    `stepchild`
    `grandchild`
    `brother`
    `sister`
    `half-brother`
    `half-sister`
    `another relationship`

-- A "means" definition: JUST FALSE when no head holds, and for a partner whose relationship did not exist when the
-- wrongful act, neglect or default occurred.
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased`
      `the personal relationship existed when the wrongful act occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3 -- I am a member of the family` `my relationship` `the personal relationship existed when the wrongful act occurred` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my relationship`
    WHEN `partner in a personal relationship` THEN
        IF `the personal relationship existed when the wrongful act occurred`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `another relationship` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

GIVEN `the answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is yes` `the answer` IF
    CONSIDER `the answer`
    WHEN JUST b THEN b
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 4 -- the action survives the death`

-- s 4: "Whenever the death of a person is caused by wrongful act, neglect, or default, and the act, neglect, or default
-- is such as would, if death had not ensued, have entitled the party injured to maintain an action and recover damages
-- in respect thereof, then and in every such case the person who would have been liable if death had not ensued shall
-- be liable to an action for damages, notwithstanding the death of the person injured, and although the death shall
-- have been caused under such circumstances as amount in law to a crime." A "whenever" condition counts as "where"
-- (rulings of 2026-11-05): JUST FALSE when it fails. Whether there was a wrongful act, neglect or default, and whether
-- the deceased could have sued, are the court's findings, asked as facts. That the death was a crime changes nothing,
-- and so is not asked. s 2(2): the Crown may be sued "in the same circumstances as ... a subject".
GIVEN `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the deceased could have sued for it had death not ensued` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4 -- the wrongdoer is liable to an action for the death` `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` `the deceased could have sued for it had death not ensued` MEANS
    IF `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default`
       AND `the deceased could have sued for it had death not ensued`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 5, 6 -- an action for my benefit`

-- s 5: "Every such action shall be for the benefit of the members of the family of the persons whose death shall have
-- been so caused". s 6(1): "Not more than one action under this Act shall lie for and in respect of the same
-- subject-matter of complaint." A "means" definition (s 3), a "whenever" (s 4) and a flat negative (s 6): every no is
-- JUST FALSE.
GIVEN `I am a member of the family` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the wrongdoer is liable` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`ss 4, 5, 6 -- an action lies for my benefit` `I am a member of the family` `the wrongdoer is liable` `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death` MEANS
    IF `it is yes` `I am a member of the family`
       AND `it is yes` `the wrongdoer is liable`
       AND (NOT `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 5, 8 -- who brings the action`

-- s 5: the action "subject to this Act, shall be brought by and in the name of the executor or administrator of the
-- person deceased". s 8(1): "If in any case there is no executor or administrator of the person deceased, or, if there
-- is such executor or administrator and no action under this Act is brought within 6 months after the death of such
-- deceased person by and in the name of his executor or administrator, then and in every such case such action may be
-- brought by and in the name or names of all or any of the persons ... for whose benefit such action would have been".
-- (2): such an action "shall be for the benefit of the same persons, and shall be subject to the same regulations and
-- procedure".
--
-- The regulative rule: while there is an executor or administrator, the 6 months after the death are theirs, and a
-- member of the family shall not bring the action in his or her own name until they have passed. The 6 months end on the
-- corresponding day 6 months after the death (fork F1); the rule starts on the day of the death, so a step on day n is
-- at time n. FULFILLED means the step was taken after the 6 months; BREACH means it was taken within them. It takes a
-- DEONTIC value, so it is never exported.
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `a member of the family`
    `the plaintiff`

DECLARE `Step` IS ONE OF
    `bring the action in my own name`
    `deliver the statement of claim`
    `deliver full particulars`

GIVEN `the days of the executor's 6 months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 8(1) -- the executor's 6 months` `the days of the executor's 6 months` MEANS
    PARTY `a member of the family`
    SHANT `bring the action in my own name`
    WITHIN `the days of the executor's 6 months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `a member of the family` BECAUSE "within 6 months after the death, while there is an executor or administrator, the action is to be brought by and in the name of the executor or administrator (ss 5, 8(1))"

GIVEN `the day of the death` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 8(1) -- the last day of the 6 months after` `the day of the death` MEANS `the corresponding day` 6 `months after` `the day of the death`

GIVEN `the day I bring it` IS A DATE, `the day of the death` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `bringing it on` `the day I bring it` `is after the executor's 6 months from` `the day of the death` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 8(1) -- the executor's 6 months` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time of the step`), (EVENT `a member of the family` `bring the action in my own name` `the time of the step`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `the day of the death` `to` (`s 8(1) -- the last day of the 6 months after` `the day of the death`)
        `the time of the step` MEANS `the days from` `the day of the death` `to` `the day I bring it`

-- Whether I may bring the action in my own name on a day. "persons ... for whose benefit" (s 8(1)) are the members of the
-- family (s 5): JUST FALSE for anyone else. s 6: JUST FALSE once an action has been brought. s 8(1)'s "if" conditions:
-- JUST TRUE where there is no executor or administrator; otherwise JUST TRUE only after the 6 months (an "if" that fails:
-- JUST FALSE). Whether there is an executor or administrator is read on the day I bring the action (fork F2).
GIVEN `I am a member of the family` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `there is an executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the death` IS A DATE
      `the day I bring it` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8(1) -- I may bring the action in my own name on that day` `I am a member of the family` `there is an executor or administrator` `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death` `the day of the death` `the day I bring it` MEANS
    IF NOT (`it is yes` `I am a member of the family`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF NOT `there is an executor or administrator`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE IF `bringing it on` `the day I bring it` `is after the executor's 6 months from` `the day of the death`
                   THEN JUST TRUE
                   ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Own-name position` IS ONE OF
    `you may bring it now, as there is no executor or administrator`
    `you may bring it from` HAS `the first day` IS A DATE
    `no, an action for this death has already been brought`
    `no, you are not a member of the family for whose benefit it is brought`

GIVEN `I am a member of the family` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `there is an executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the death` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Own-name position`
`s 8(1) -- when I may bring the action in my own name` `I am a member of the family` `there is an executor or administrator` `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death` `the day of the death` MEANS
    IF NOT (`it is yes` `I am a member of the family`)
    THEN `no, you are not a member of the family for whose benefit it is brought`
    ELSE IF `an action under this Act has already been brought for this death`
         THEN `no, an action for this death has already been brought`
         ELSE IF NOT `there is an executor or administrator`
              THEN `you may bring it now, as there is no executor or administrator`
              ELSE `you may bring it from` (`the day after` (`s 8(1) -- the last day of the 6 months after` `the day of the death`))

§§ `s 7 -- particulars`

-- s 7: "In every such action the plaintiff on the record shall be required, together with the statement of claim, to
-- deliver to the defendant or the defendant's Australian legal practitioner a full particular of the persons for whom and
-- on whose behalf such action is brought, and of the nature of the claim in respect of which damages are sought to be
-- recovered." The regulative rule: once the statement of claim is delivered, full particulars must follow WITHIN 0 days
-- ("together with": the same day, fork F3). Particulars count only if delivered to the defendant or the defendant's
-- Australian legal practitioner and if they cover both the persons and the nature of the claim.
DECLARE `Delivered to` IS ONE OF
    `the defendant`
    `the defendant's Australian legal practitioner`
    `someone else`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 7 -- particulars with the statement of claim` MEANS
    PARTY `the plaintiff`
    MUST `deliver the statement of claim`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the plaintiff`
        MUST `deliver full particulars`
        WITHIN 0
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY `the plaintiff` BECAUSE "full particulars of the persons for whom the action is brought and of the nature of the claim were not delivered together with the statement of claim (s 7)"

GIVEN `who received them` IS A `Delivered to`
      `they name every person for whom the action is brought` IS A BOOLEAN
      `they state the nature of the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `they are full particulars delivered as s 7 requires` `who received them` `they name every person for whom the action is brought` `they state the nature of the claim` IF
    `they name every person for whom the action is brought`
    AND `they state the nature of the claim`
    AND (NOT (`who received them` EQUALS `someone else`))

-- NOTHING before the statement of claim is delivered (the duty has not arisen: a missing event, decided case by case,
-- fork F4); then JUST TRUE if the trace is FULFILLED, JUST FALSE otherwise (a "shall be required" not done, fork F4).
-- Particulars delivered before the statement of claim are not matched by the rule, which is waiting for the statement.
GIVEN `the statement of claim has been delivered` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it was delivered` IS A DATE
      `particulars have been delivered` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day they were delivered` IS A DATE
      `who received them` IS A `Delivered to`
      `they name every person for whom the action is brought` IS A BOOLEAN
      `they state the nature of the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 7 -- I have delivered the particulars required` `the statement of claim has been delivered` `the day it was delivered` `particulars have been delivered` `the day they were delivered` `who received them` `they name every person for whom the action is brought` `they state the nature of the claim` MEANS
    IF NOT `the statement of claim has been delivered`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 7 -- particulars with the statement of claim` 0 `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST TRUE
         OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `the particulars time` MEANS `the days from` `the day it was delivered` `to` `the day they were delivered`
        `the particulars count` MEANS
            `particulars have been delivered`
            AND `they are full particulars delivered as s 7 requires` `who received them` `they name every person for whom the action is brought` `they state the nature of the claim`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `the particulars count` AND `the particulars time` AT LEAST 0
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the plaintiff` `deliver the statement of claim` 0), (WAITUNTIL `the particulars time`), (EVENT `the plaintiff` `deliver full particulars` `the particulars time`), (WAITUNTIL (`the particulars time` PLUS 1))
            ELSE LIST (EVENT `the plaintiff` `deliver the statement of claim` 0), (WAITUNTIL 1)

§§ `s 9 -- payment into court`

-- s 9(1): the defendant may pay money into court "as compensation in one sum to all persons entitled under this Act ...
-- without specifying the shares". (2): "If the sum so paid into court is not accepted, and an issue is taken by the
-- plaintiff as to its sufficiency, and the jury shall think the sum sufficient, the defendant shall be entitled to the
-- verdict upon that issue." An "if" condition: JUST FALSE (this section gives the defendant no verdict) when it fails.
-- Whether the jury think the sum sufficient is their judgment, asked as a fact.
GIVEN `money has been paid into court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was accepted` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the plaintiff took issue as to its sufficiency` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the jury think the sum sufficient` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(2) -- the defendant is entitled to the verdict on that issue` `money has been paid into court` `it was accepted` `the plaintiff took issue as to its sufficiency` `the jury think the sum sufficient` MEANS
    IF `money has been paid into court`
       AND (NOT `it was accepted`)
       AND `the plaintiff took issue as to its sufficiency`
       AND `the jury think the sum sufficient`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 10(1) -- what is not taken into account`

-- s 10(1)(b): the estate passing to the family is not taken into account "except so far as that value exceeds the sum of
-- $250 000".
`s 10(1)(b) -- the estate threshold` MEANS 250000

-- "the value of such portion of the estate of the deceased person as passes to the members of his family": the whole
-- portion passing to all of them, not each member's part (finding 4).
GIVEN `the value of the estate passing to the family` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 10(1)(b) -- the value that may be taken into account` `the value of the estate passing to the family` MEANS
    max 0 (`the value of the estate passing to the family` MINUS `s 10(1)(b) -- the estate threshold`)

-- s 10(1): "In assessing damages in any action under this Act, there shall not be taken into account -- (a) any sum paid
-- or payable on the death of the deceased under any contract of insurance or assurance ...; (b) [the estate, above];
-- (c) any sum paid or payable out of any superannuation, provident, or like fund or by way of benefit from a friendly
-- society, benefit society, or trade union ...; or (d) any sum paid or payable by way of pension, benefit, or allowance
-- under -- (i) the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986 of the Commonwealth; or (ii) the Social Security Act 1991 of the
-- Commonwealth -- or under any Act ... amending or replacing any such Act."
DECLARE `Payment or benefit` IS ONE OF
    `a sum under a contract of insurance or assurance paid or payable on the death`
    `a sum from a superannuation, provident or like fund`
    `a benefit from a friendly society, benefit society or trade union`
    `a pension, benefit or allowance under the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986`
    `a pension, benefit or allowance under the Social Security Act 1991`
    `the part of the estate that passes to members of the family`
    `another payment or benefit`

-- "shall not be taken into account": a flat negative, JUST FALSE. The estate: JUST TRUE for the value above $250 000,
-- JUST FALSE at or below it ("except so far as"). Another payment: the Act is silent and the common law decides
-- (NOTHING-kind: the text is silent).
GIVEN `what it is` IS A `Payment or benefit`
      `the value of the estate passing to the family` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 10(1) -- it may be taken into account in assessing damages` `what it is` `the value of the estate passing to the family` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what it is`
    WHEN `the part of the estate that passes to members of the family` THEN
        IF `s 10(1)(b) -- the value that may be taken into account` `the value of the estate passing to the family` GREATER THAN 0
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `another payment or benefit` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 10(2) -- funeral, headstone, hospital and medical expenses`

-- s 10(2): "damages may be awarded in respect of the expenses of the funeral or cremation of the deceased person and the
-- reasonable cost of erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave of the deceased person and hospital and medical
-- expenses incurred as a result of the wrongful act causing his death if such expenses have been incurred or paid by the
-- parties by whom, or for whose benefit, the action is brought, whether or not any such parties have sustained other
-- pecuniary loss by reason of the death of the deceased."
DECLARE `Expense` IS ONE OF
    `funeral or cremation expenses`
    `the cost of erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave`
    `hospital and medical expenses`
    `another expense`

-- The most s 10(2) lets the damages include for one expense. "if such expenses have been incurred or paid by the
-- parties": when that fails, s 10(2) gives nothing (JUST 0); so too for hospital and medical expenses not "incurred as a
-- result of the wrongful act causing his death" (a limit in the text's own words read with the "if": fork F5). Only the
-- headstone is limited to its "reasonable cost", entered as the court would find it; the award is the lower of what was
-- paid and that cost (fork F6). Another expense: s 10(2) is silent (NOTHING-kind: the text is silent; the general damages
-- of s 5 may still reach it).
GIVEN `the expense` IS AN `Expense`
      `the amount incurred or paid` IS A NUMBER
      `it was incurred or paid by a party by whom or for whose benefit the action is brought` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was incurred as a result of the wrongful act causing the death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the reasonable cost of the headstone` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 10(2) -- the damages that may be awarded for the expense` `the expense` `the amount incurred or paid` `it was incurred or paid by a party by whom or for whose benefit the action is brought` `it was incurred as a result of the wrongful act causing the death` `the reasonable cost of the headstone` MEANS
    IF `the expense` EQUALS `another expense`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF NOT `it was incurred or paid by a party by whom or for whose benefit the action is brought`
         THEN JUST 0
         ELSE CONSIDER `the expense`
              WHEN `funeral or cremation expenses` THEN JUST `the amount incurred or paid`
              WHEN `the cost of erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave` THEN JUST (min `the amount incurred or paid` `the reasonable cost of the headstone`)
              WHEN `hospital and medical expenses` THEN
                  IF `it was incurred as a result of the wrongful act causing the death`
                  THEN JUST `the amount incurred or paid`
                  ELSE JUST 0
              OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `s 5 -- dividing the amount recovered`

-- s 5: "the amount so recovered, after deducting the costs not recovered from the defendant, shall be divided amongst the
-- parties aforesaid in such shares as the jury by their verdicts shall find and direct." The shares are the jury's,
-- entered as amounts; the costs not recovered are borne in proportion to the shares and never take the amount below
-- nothing (fork F7).
GIVEN `the amount recovered` IS A NUMBER
      `the costs not recovered from the defendant` IS A NUMBER
      `the share the jury directed for me` IS A NUMBER
      `the total of all the shares the jury directed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 5 -- my part of the amount recovered` `the amount recovered` `the costs not recovered from the defendant` `the share the jury directed for me` `the total of all the shares the jury directed` MEANS
    IF `the total of all the shares the jury directed` AT MOST 0
    THEN 0
    ELSE `the amount to divide` TIMES (`the share the jury directed for me` DIVIDED BY `the total of all the shares the jury directed`)
    WHERE
        `the amount to divide` MEANS max 0 (`the amount recovered` MINUS `the costs not recovered from the defendant`)
